<h2>Disposable, rechargeable or reusable?</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Air-activated disposables</strong> (Ignik, HotHands) start when exposed to air and usually last 7 to 10 hours. They're cheap, thin enough for gloves and boots, and single-use.</li>
  <li><strong>Rechargeables</strong> get hotter and cut waste, but cold drains batteries, they weigh more, and some take 3 to 4 hours to charge (GearJunkie). Use the cloth pouch: Outdoor Life notes some get hot enough to burn.</li>
  <li><strong>Reusable gel packs</strong> (sodium acetate) heat instantly but last only an hour or two and need boiling to reset.</li>
  <li><strong>Catalytic warmers</strong> run longest on lighter fluid. Use the right fuel and avoid confined spaces (carbon monoxide).</li>
</ul>
<h2>Flying with hand warmers</h2>
<p>TSA allows hand warmers in carry-on and checked bags. Lighter fluid isn't allowed in either, so you can't fly with fuel for a catalytic warmer. Rechargeable warmers that work as power banks count as spare lithium batteries: carry-on only, up to 100 watt-hours.</p>
<p>For all-day warmth, pair them with <%= link_to "heated gloves", guide_path("best-heated-gloves") %> or <%= link_to "warm ski gloves or mittens", guide_path("ski-gloves-vs-mittens") %>, and see <%= link_to "the best heated socks", guide_path("best-heated-socks") %> for cold toes.</p>

